PR #2597  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Zicfiss shadow stack depends on Zaamo atomics; omitting Zaamo makes SSP updates non-atomic, enabling races that corrupt the shadow stack and defeat CFI
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:690-693  hart_detect_features
      The 'Extensions implied by other extensions and features' block currently only propagates mhpm_mask → ZIHPM. There is no Zicfiss → Zaamo dependency propagation. The RISC-V ISA manual requires Zaamo (atomic memory operations) as a dependency of Zicfiss (shadow stack). Without this check, a platform with Zicfiss in the DT but lacking Zaamo on silicon will silently expose shadow-stack functionality (SSP updates, shadow stack memory writes) that must be atomic but are not. The insertion point is exactly here — lines 690-693 — the existing pattern for transitive dependencies.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-isa-manual/pr-2597/

  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_fwft.c:208-215  fwft_sstack_supported
      This gate function checks only SBI_HART_EXT_ZICFISS before declaring shadow-stack support. It does not check for Zaamo. This is the S-mode-facing gate — without a Zaamo check here, an S-mode caller can enable SSE (shadow stack enable) in menvcfg on a platform lacking Zaamo, even if the hart_detect_features fix is separately applied. Platforms could theoretically bypass hart_detect_features via sbi_platform_extensions_init. Defense-in-depth demands a second check here.
  [BUG] lib/utils/fdt/fdt_helper.c:400-409  fdt_parse_isa_extensions_one_hart
      This function iterates over all known extensions and sets bits for any found in the DT ISA string with zero cross-extension validation. If a DT lists 'zicfiss' but omits 'zaamo', Zicfiss is set in the extensions bitmap silently. No dependency validation loop exists after the bit-setting loop. This is the input-validation layer — a misconfigured device tree propagates the gap unchecked into the extension bitmap consumed by all downstream query functions. A second-pass dependency validation loop after the bit-setting loop would catch misconfigured DTs at boot time.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/riscv_atomic.c:15-17  (compile-time guard)
      The #error guard checks for __riscv_atomic, __riscv_zaamo, or __riscv_zalrsc toolchain defines. This is a compile-time assertion on the toolchain target, NOT a runtime hardware capability check. A platform built with -march=rv64g (where 'g' = 'imafd', and 'a' implies atomics) compiles fine because the toolchain defines __riscv_atomic, but the actual silicon may lack Zaamo. This explains why the compile-time guard alone is insufficient to protect the shadow-stack path. The guard is useful as a build-time sanity check but cannot replace runtime dependency enforcement.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_fwft.c:79-97  fwft_menvcfg_set_bit
      The PR description claims csr_set/csr_clear 'decompose into CSR read + modify + CSR write — non-atomic on hardware without Zaamo'. This is incorrect. csr_set maps to the csrs instruction (lines 131-138 of riscv_asm.h) and csr_clear maps to csrc — both are single atomic CSR bit-set/bit-clear instructions defined by the RISC-V privileged spec. The RISC-V spec explicitly states: 'The CSRRS and CSRRC instructions are atomic read-modify-write operations on the CSR.' This atomicity is part of the base Zicsr extension and does NOT depend on Zaamo. While inter-hart CSR races are a general concern (requiring locks or IPIs), they are orthogonal to the Zaamo dependency requirement. The function is compliant with respect to the Zicfiss→Zaamo dependency spec.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:351-361  sbi_hart_has_extension
      This is a simple bit-test function that queries the extensions bitmap. By design it performs no dependency awareness — it is a plain lookup. The function itself is correct. The problem is upstream: the bitmap was populated without Zicfiss→Zaamo dependency propagation. Fixing hart_detect_features (lines 690-693) makes this function return correct results for both Zicfiss and Zaamo automatically. Marking as compliant because there is no bug here — the symptom originates elsewhere.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:363-399  sbi_hart_ext
      The __SBI_HART_EXT_DATA macro (line 363-366) stores only .name and .id fields. The extension table has no mechanism to declare 'depends_on' relationships. Zicfiss is registered at line 391 as a completely standalone extension with no dependency metadata. This is the structural root cause: without dependency information in the extension definitions, every validation site (hart_detect_features, fdt_helper, fwft checks) must independently hardcode dependency logic. Adding a .depends_on field (e.g., a bitmask of prerequisite extensions) would allow all three fix locations to use data-driven validation instead of ad-hoc checks, preventing future gaps when new extensions with dependencies are added.

